    category: 'Romance',
    excerpt: 'The sky over Boulevard Saint-Germain went from pale grey to charcoal black in three minutes. When the heavens tore open, Sophie dove beneath the red canvas awning of Café de Flore.',
    body: [
      'The sky over Boulevard Saint-Germain went from pale grey to charcoal black in three minutes. When the heavens tore open with thunder, Sophie dove beneath the red canvas awning of Café de Flore, clutching an armful of wet architectural sketches against her coat.',
      'She collided straight into a man in a navy wool trench coat holding two steaming double espressos.',
      '“Watch out—!” he laughed, catching her wrist with remarkable reflexes before her sketches could hit the streaming gutter. One of the small white porcelain cups clattered on the saucer, splashing dark liquid, but not a single drop touched her paper.',
      '“Pardonnez-moi!” Sophie gasped, brushing wet curls away from her forehead. “I didn’t see you.”',
      '“Clearly,” he grinned, his hazel eyes crinkling at the corners. He offered her the unspilled cup with an effortless bow. “Take it. You look like you’ve just survived a shipwreck in the 6th arrondissement.”',
      'His name was Gabriel, a landscape architect from Lyon who was in Paris for three days to present a park restoration proposal. As the deluge transformed the cobbled street into a rushing river, they pulled two wicker bistro chairs close together under the canvas overhang.',
      'What was supposed to be a five-minute storm lasted two hours. They debated whether Haussmann ruined medieval Paris, shared a warm pain au chocolat, and discovered that both of them had grown up reading the same obscure travel memoirs by Nicolas Bouvier.',
      'By the time the clouds parted and golden late-afternoon sunlight bathed the wet zinc rooftops in liquid amber, the waiter arrived with their bill.',
      'Gabriel looked at the clearing sky with genuine disappointment. “The rain stopped,” he said quietly.',
      'Sophie looked at him, slipped her sketches into her leather satchel, and smiled. “Only on this street. I hear Montmartre is still expecting thunderstorms. Walk with me?”'
    ],
    reflection: 'How often do we view interruptions and sudden storms as nuisances, instead of the very doorway through which unexpected joy arrives?'
