    excerpt: 'The suite at the Hôtel de Paris smelled of bitter almonds, expensive cigars, and sea salt blowing off the Mediterranean harbor.',
    body: [
      'Suite 504 at the Hôtel de Paris smelled of three distinct things: bitter almonds, vintage Cuban tobacco, and the sea spray rolling into Monaco from the Mediterranean. On the silk settee sat Baron von Heller, slumped backward with a gin martini glass shattered between his patent leather shoes.',
      'Chief Inspector Laurent of the Sûreté had already labeled it suicide. I knew better the moment I examined the Baron’s left lapel.',
      '“A man committing suicide does not pin fresh white gardenias to his dinner jacket three minutes before swallowing cyanide,” I observed, adjusting my cuffs. “And he certainly doesn’t bother winning eighty thousand francs at the baccarat tables downstairs.”',
      'Across the marble fireplace stood Madame Vivienne Saint-Clair, wrapped in an emerald evening gown that cost more than my precinct’s annual budget. She was smoking through an ivory holder, watching me like a panther sizing up an uninvited guest.',
      '“The Baron was known for theatrical gestures, Monsieur Fontaine,” she purred, her French accent dripping with aristocratic boredom. “Perhaps he simply wanted to depart in style.”',
      '“Or perhaps,” I said, stepping closer to her, “someone swapped his pillbox when he leaned over to light your cigarette at midnight. The imperial jade pendant he was smuggling from Tangier — the one that disappeared from his safe fifteen minutes ago — wouldn’t happen to be resting in your beaded clutch, would it?”',
      'Her eyes narrowed. For a second, the mask of society elegance slipped, revealing the razor-sharp mind of Europe’s most elusive art thief.',
      '“You’re smarter than you look, Fontaine,” she whispered, slipping one hand into her clutch.',
      'I smiled, tapping the pocket of my tuxedo. “And you’re less observant than usual, Madame. The harbor police boat you arranged for 2:00 AM? I had the harbormaster impound it twenty minutes ago. Shall we walk down together, or do you prefer the service elevator?”'
    ],
    reflection: 'In high-stakes games of wits, the person who believes they are untouchable is always the first one to make a mistake.'
