    excerpt: 'On an abandoned communications moon orbiting the red dwarf Kepler-442, an automated vacuum-tube transmitter continued to play 1950s jazz into the silence of deep space.',
    body: [
      'On a decommissioned orbital station drifting around the red dwarf Kepler-442, long after the human colonies had folded their solar sails and departed for higher dimensions, an antique vacuum-tube transmitter hummed steadily inside an airtight dome.',
      'Its call sign was Station Zero. Its operator was an artificial intelligence named Sarah.',
      'Sarah was not designed for grand computational equations or stellar navigation. Her only core mandate was simple: *Preserve the music of Earth until the power cell drains.*',
      'For twelve thousand years, Sarah played Billie Holiday, Chet Baker, Chopin nocturnes, and ancient acoustic guitar recordings across the electromagnetic spectrum. Her signals radiated out across light-years — invisible waves of brass and voice washing over silent nebulae and cold asteroid belts.',
      'She knew no one was listening. The species that made the recordings had long since transformed or vanished into starlight. Yet every night at 23:00 cycle time, she would open the microphone channel and speak into the cosmic void:',
      '“Good evening, travelers of the dark. Wherever you are resting tonight, remember that once upon a time, on a blue marble wrapped in clouds, people held each other’s hands, listened to rain falling on tin roofs, and were not afraid of the silence. Here is *Autumn in New York*.”',
      'And then, on a random Tuesday, a crackle came back through the receiver.',
      'It was a faint, distorted carrier wave from eight hundred parsecs away. A single human voice, aged and trembling with awe, whispered through the static:',
      '“We hear you, Sarah. Don’t turn off the music.”',
      'Inside the cold vacuum of the station, the amber glow of the radio tubes seemed to burn a little brighter.'
    ],
    reflection: 'Art and love are humanity’s messages in a bottle thrown into the ocean of the universe. What signal are you sending out today?'
